Libraries and databases use structured records

Modern databases extend the logic of the catalogue card. Records contain searchable fields such as:

title and abstract;
author and affiliation;
keywords and controlled subject terms;
publication year and document type;
references and citation links.


The catalogue cards in this image made books discoverable through consistent descriptions, classification, and cross-references. Academic databases apply the same principle digitally and at a much larger scale, so your query interacts with structured fields rather than an undifferentiated pile of PDFs. Scopus, Web of Science, CAB Abstracts, PubMed, IEEE Xplore, ProQuest, institutional repositories, and government databases serve different disciplinary needs. Their coverage differs, which is why one database rarely provides a complete picture of an evidence base.

A good question is answerable and consequential
Check that the question is:

clear enough to interpret consistently;
bounded by population, setting, and time;
searchable through recognisable concepts;
measurable through defined outcomes;
feasible within available evidence and resources;
worth answering for scholarship or practice.


Use this checklist to diagnose your question. A question may be precise yet unimportant, or important yet impossible to answer with the available evidence. Your preliminary search may reveal a need for revision: many irrelevant results can indicate ambiguity, while very few results can indicate an overly narrow formulation or unfamiliar vocabulary. Revising your question demonstrates learning as your understanding improves. The components of the refined question now become the building blocks of the search.

A mini-protocol fixes decisions before results
A practical protocol records:

the question and scope;
information sources and search strings;
date, language, and document-type limits;
inclusion and exclusion criteria;
screening stages and decision rules;
extraction fields and quality checks;
planned synthesis approach.


Your protocol protects the review from decisions that shift in response to attractive findings. For a taught review, the protocol may fit on one or two pages because prospective clarity matters more than length. A pilot search may reveal that a planned criterion is impossible or ambiguous, and you can amend the protocol when you record both the change and its reason. This creates an honest audit trail showing how the method developed.

Record full-text exclusion reasons
Common reasons include:

wrong population or setting;
wrong intervention or exposure;
outcome not reported;
unsuitable study design;
duplicate or secondary report;
insufficient methodological information;
inaccessible full text after reasonable attempts.


One primary reason for each excluded full text usually produces the clearest audit trail. Your reasons should be mutually understandable and connected to the eligibility criteria. If access is the problem, record it honestly rather than treating the paper as irrelevant. A systematic review may display selection through a flow diagram, while a smaller assignment may use a concise table. In either case, every study in the final evidence set should be traceable to the original search results.

Pilot the screening rules
Before screening hundreds of records:

select a varied sample of 10-20 records;
apply the criteria independently or twice at different times;
identify ambiguous wording;
revise the decision rules;
document the revision and begin formal screening.


Piloting provides quality control because ambiguities are easier to resolve before full screening. If you are working with another reviewer, compare decisions and discuss the rule behind each disagreement. If you are working independently, repeat a small sample after a short interval and check your consistency. Your criteria should stabilise before formal screening, although genuinely unforeseen issues may require documented amendments. Inclusion establishes relevance, and our next task is to examine credibility through critical appraisal.

CLT demo (R): start with a skewed distribution
We’ll sample from an exponential distribution (skewed right), then look at the distribution of sample means.


Code
set.seed(4013)

n_sims &lt;- 6000
ns &lt;- c(1, 2, 5, 10, 30, 100)

means &lt;- map_dfr(ns, function(n){
  tibble(
    n = n,
    xbar = replicate(n_sims, mean(rexp(n, rate = 1)))
  )
})

# For Exponential(rate=1): mu=1, sigma=1, so sd(xbar)=1/sqrt(n)
curve_df &lt;- means |&gt;
  group_by(n) |&gt;
  summarise(
    xmin = quantile(xbar, 0.001),
    xmax = quantile(xbar, 0.999),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(n) |&gt;
  summarise(
    x = list(seq(xmin, xmax, length.out = 250)),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  tidyr::unnest(x) |&gt;
  mutate(
    mu = 1,
    sd = 1 / sqrt(n),
    y = dnorm(x, mean = mu, sd = sd)
  )

ggplot(means, aes(x = xbar)) +
  geom_histogram(aes(y = after_stat(density)), bins = 45, fill = "grey80", color = "white") +
  geom_line(data = curve_df, aes(x = x, y = y), color = "#6D28D9", linewidth = 1) +
  facet_wrap(~ n, scales = "free", ncol = 3) +
  labs(
    title = "Central Limit Theorem: sample means become approximately normal",
    subtitle = "Data are exponential (skewed). Purple curve is the normal approximation for the mean.",
    x = "Sample mean (x̄)",
    y = "Density"
  )

Task A solution
# Solution for Task A
set.seed(123)  # For reproducibility

diamonds_miss &lt;- diamonds |&gt;
  mutate(
    price = ifelse(runif(n()) &lt; 0.05, NA, price),  # 5% missing in price
    carat = ifelse(runif(n()) &lt; 0.05, NA, carat)   # 5% missing in carat
  ) |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(price), !is.na(carat)) |&gt;
  mutate(price_capped = pmin(price, 15000)) |&gt;
  group_by(cut) |&gt;
  summarise(
    mean_capped_price = mean(price_capped),
    median_capped_price = median(price_capped),
    mean_carat = mean(carat),
    count = n(),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = cut, y = mean_capped_price, fill = cut)) +
  geom_col(show.legend = FALSE) +
  labs(
    title = "Mean capped price by cut",
    x = "Cut",
    y = "Mean capped price"
  )

Correlation and causation
An association does not by itself establish that one variable causes the other.
Possible explanations include:

a direct causal effect;
reverse causation;
a third variable influencing both measurements;
selection or measurement processes;
coincidence within a small sample.

Conditions for Pearson correlation
Pearson correlation is most informative when:

both variables are quantitative;
observations are independent;
the relationship is approximately linear;
influential outliers are absent or scientifically justified;
uncertainty is estimated under a suitable distributional model.

Choosing and reporting
Choose Pearson when the scientific question concerns a linear relationship and its assumptions are reasonable. Choose Spearman or Kendall when ranks and monotonic association better represent the variables and question.
A concise report should include:

the variables and sample size;
the selected coefficient and reason for choosing it;
the estimated coefficient;
uncertainty and p-value where appropriate;
a graph and a contextual interpretation;
a clear statement that association alone does not establish causation.

Simulating the t-distribution

simulate_t &lt;- function(n, reps = 10000) {
  t_values &lt;- numeric(reps)
  for (i in seq_len(reps)) {
    x &lt;- rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = 1)
    xbar &lt;- mean(x)
    s &lt;- sd(x)
    t_values[i] &lt;- (xbar - 0) / (s / sqrt(n))
  }
  data.frame(t = t_values)
}
t_df_5 &lt;- simulate_t(n = 5)

Task 5.4: Perform a reproducibility check
Exchange the protocol with a peer. Ask them to identify:

the exact population, phenomenon, comparison, and outcomes;
the sources and date limits;
the exact search to run;
the rules for retaining a record;
the information to extract from an included paper.

Revise every instruction that requires the peer to guess.






NoteExpected output from Section 5



Save a populated search-log row, a one-page mini-protocol, and a clear folder or filename plan. Another student should be able to repeat the search from these records.








ImportantCheck your records



Separate your planned decisions from your observed outcomes. Your protocol says what you will do and why, while your search log records what you actually did and what occurred. Record any differences transparently as amendments.

Week 1 practical portfolio
By the end of the eight sections, your portfolio should contain:

a review purpose and provisional knowledge map;
a source map and vocabulary bank;
a structured question and eligibility criteria;
a tested concept table and search string;
a search log and mini-protocol;
a pilot screening record and selection funnel;
a critical appraisal with evidential weight; and
an extraction matrix, thematic grouping, and paragraph plan.

Together, these outputs form the working foundation of a credible literature review. They also preserve the reasoning behind the review, allowing us to explain how the final written argument developed from a focused question and a transparent chain of evidence.

5.2 Proportional stratified sampling


Code
n_total &lt;- 20

stratum_counts &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  count(stratum_ns) |&gt;
  mutate(prop = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
  mutate(n_stratum = round(prop * n_total))

stratum_counts




Code
strat_sample_ids &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  left_join(stratum_counts, by = "stratum_ns") |&gt;
  group_by(stratum_ns) |&gt;
  group_modify(~ slice_sample(.x, n = .x$n_stratum[1])) |&gt;
  pull(cell_id)

strat_cells &lt;- grid_sf |&gt;
  filter(cell_id %in% strat_sample_ids)

strat_points &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  filter(cell_id %in% strat_sample_ids)

strat_cells




Code
ggplot() +
  geom_sf(data = grid_sf, fill = NA, colour = "grey90") +
  geom_sf(data = strat_cells, fill = NA, colour = "darkgreen") +
  geom_sf(data = strat_points, aes(colour = stratum_ns), size = 2) +
  ggtitle("Stratified Sample") +
  theme_minimal()

6.2 Sampling clusters


Code
n_clusters_to_sample &lt;- 3
n_cells_per_cluster  &lt;- 3

clusters_to_sample &lt;- cluster_summary |&gt;
  slice_sample(n = n_clusters_to_sample) |&gt;
  pull(cluster_id)

clusters_to_sample




Code
cluster_sample_ids &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  filter(cluster_id %in% clusters_to_sample) |&gt;
  group_by(cluster_id) |&gt;
  slice_sample(n = n_cells_per_cluster) |&gt;
  pull(cell_id)

cluster_cells &lt;- grid_sf |&gt;
  filter(cell_id %in% cluster_sample_ids)

cluster_points &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  filter(cell_id %in% cluster_sample_ids)

cluster_cells




Code
all_cells_in_sampled_clusters &lt;- grid_centroids |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  filter(cluster_id %in% clusters_to_sample) |&gt;
  pull(cell_id)

clusters_all_cells &lt;- grid_sf |&gt;
  filter(cell_id %in% all_cells_in_sampled_clusters)


ggplot() +
  geom_sf(data = grid_sf, fill = NA, colour = "grey90") +
  geom_sf(data = clusters_all_cells, fill = NA, colour = "orange") +
  geom_sf(data = cluster_cells, fill = NA, colour = "red") +
  geom_sf(data = cluster_points, colour = "red", size = 2) +
  ggtitle("Cluster Sampling") +
  theme_minimal()
